ops.note:
I concur, and I have no veto to invoke. My record is Global Services, not Commercial Airplanes. The KPI gate, the strain test and the supplier gate below come from doctrine, not from my record.

First and foremost, the KPI gate on the Rate Increase (doctrine) [B-2221, B-2351, B-2308]. The CEO ordered rate_737: hold, so there is no Rate Increase for my veto to act on. I also re-read round1.md: it reports no live quality, FAA or supply-chain problem, so the veto could not have bound in any case. The hold matches my capacity-follows-demand result, which is an inference from one remark [BX-1329]. At 57 a month, 66, 42 and 20 MAX slots sit idle in 2032-34. Against the same plan without the step, the item value is -0.21 / -0.56 / -0.83 / -0.21 / -0.57 / -0.56 (-0.56 in S2). The option lapses after this round, which keeps $0.56B in S2.

Second, engineering capacity (doctrine; board arithmetic) [B-0341]. Nothing is launched, so there is no overlap and no strain this round, and the package breaks no hard rule. Holding the 787 Re-engine is the CEO's call on the odds of an A350 Re-engine. It is not an operations finding: the Re-engine passes every operations test. It has zero overlap alone, costs about $1B a year (7% of base free cash flow), holds net debt at $0.0B in stress, and has a GEnx-derivative route to 2035. The cost of waiting is now on the record. A 2035 Re-engine overlaps a 2035 10-year fps by 2 years ($1.2B of strain undiscounted, within H4) and rules out a same-round 7-year fps (5 years of overlap, barred by H4).

And finally, disciplined profitable growth [BX-1327, BX-1330]. The CEO took up my operations points for the 2035 case:
- the 10-year ramp, which beats the 7-year by $2.65-3.63B in every column;
- no via Embraer, which trails Solo by $18.05-18.06B and adds nothing Boeing lacks;
- a Renton wind-down paced to the fps ramp, because the Do Nothing backlog reaches 792 by 2047;
- supplier and LEAP-1B readiness reviews, kept as a hard gate (doctrine) [B-2308, B-2330].

For the Board: the KPI veto is not invoked. There is no red line, and all orders are at default, so no item goes to the Board.

ops.would_change_mind_if:
- Alt plan to base: any of these reported in a brief would move me back to the base plan:
  - an A320 quality escape or safety event on the A320 lines;
  - an A320neo engine supply crunch (CFM or P&W);
  - Toepfer unable to keep the $0.75B under the $5.2B line in every development year.
- NGSA launch: only a reported engine maturity slip that moves the technology-ready year past 2037 changes it. I would re-time NGSA, never cancel it. Nothing Boeing does changes it.
- Engine code: a public P&W or Rolls-Royce decision not to launch would let me narrow to code 6 or 5. That changes no payoff.
- A350 Re-engine: in round 2 I would pass a 2035 launch at the 2-year limit only if all of these hold:
  - the A350F and the Spirit A350 packages are shown to be absorbed;
  - the grid shows at least $1B;
  - cash passes;
  - no supply crunch is reported.
  Otherwise I prefer the 2045 round, and never after a 787 Re-engine.
- Delay Tactics: only an fps on the public record could reopen the question, and then only a single `poaching` move with all the Board's conditions met. The Board vetoes it now regardless.

memo_weighing.cfo:
Weighed heavily and adopted. Rahul's item values match the grid to the cent; I checked his weighted figures myself: GEnx +0.78, Ducted -0.52, Ducted vs GEnx -1.30, plan B row -21.17. Two points of his decided the round.

(1) The honest comparator for a Ducted alone is GEnx alone, not hold. That puts break-even at about 55% on S2+S5.

(2) His off-grid timing estimate puts the cost of deferring the Ducted to 2035 at $0.5-0.7B in S2 and S5. I take it as an inference, but it is under ε, and it makes waiting the better plan in every column. His veto tests (pays for itself, volumes first, premium cap) bind a Ducted now with S1 expected. My plan does not trip them, so no override is needed.

Accepted:
- R&D per year nil against about $2.5B;
- the 70% payout whole;
- no rating risk (debt $35B against an EV of $160B);
- booking low at +0.77.

Carried to 2035: his flag on whether the 2030 GEnx counts as a running project for strain. His volume dates have slipped before, and so have mine. That's another reason to wait for an airframer's volumes before committing.

